<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Dunbar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1460–c. 1520</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Dunbar was a Scottish poet at the court of James IV, the most accomplished of the makars writing in Scots. His varied output includes The Thrissil and the Rois, celebrating the king's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-tudor">Margaret Tudor</a> in 1503, comic and satirical verse and the Lament for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makari">Makaris</a>, a meditation on death that names earlier poets. He disappears from the records after 1513.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-dunbar.jpg" alt="William Dunbar" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stephencdickson · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_William_Dunbar,_Scottish_National_Portrait_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dunbar first appears in 1474 as a new student in the Faculty of Arts at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, where he took his bachelor's degree in 1477 and his master's in 1479; as students then usually entered at fourteen, his birth is put in 1459 or 1460. A satirical line in The Flyting of Dumbar and Kennedie suggests he came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a>. He was ordained a priest at an unknown date. He travelled with an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1491 and 1492, and in 1501 and 1502 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the staff of Andrew Forman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Moray.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1500 he held a pension at the court of James IV, recorded only as a servitour, and most of his poetry dates from these years. Several poems appeared in the Chepman and Myllar prints of 1508, the first books printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In 1510 his pension was set at eighty pounds Scots, three times the salary of Hector Boece as Principal of King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>. The last reliable record is a payment in May 1513; after the king's death at Flodden that September the accounts lapse, and when they resume in 1515 he is no longer listed. Sir David Lyndsay spoke of him as dead in 1530.</p><p>Many poems ask the king for advancement, above all a church benefice, as in Quone Mony Benefices Vakit, and in The Petition of The Gray Horse, Auld Dunbar he asked for a new suit of clothes for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. Meditatioun In Wyntir reflects on age and disappointed hopes, and the Lament for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makari">Makaris</a>, each verse ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> mortis conturbat me, remembers the Scots poets who had died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>About a hundred and one poems are ascribed to him, at least ninety generally accepted. Beside courtly allegory such as The Goldyn Targe, in which the dreamer fails to ward off Dame Beauty's arrows with Reason's shield, he wrote religious poems on the Nativity, Passion and Resurrection, satires on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>'s merchants, the Court of Session and the would-be aviator John Damian, and comic pieces such as The Ballad of Kynd Kittok. In the Flyting he and Walter Kennedy traded insults that also reflect the divide between the Scots of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothians</a> and Gaelic Carrick.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a> called him unrivalled among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s poets, and the Scottish Literary Renaissance of the mid-20th century, led by Hugh MacDiarmid and Sydney Goodsir Smith, took him as its model with the call to go back to Dunbar. He is commemorated in Makars' Court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Dunbar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom produced a distinctive literature in Scots, from John Barbour’s verse epic The Bruce of the 1370s to the makars of the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, Robert Henryson, <b>William Dunbar</b> and Gavin Douglas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.92,-2.75&amp;year=1499" aria-label="East Lothian on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William Dunbar"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4868 5346h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4868 5346h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.92,-2.75&amp;year=1499">What was here in 1499? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
